fetched_ \n \n [Lumibot input context pruned 15687 characters from tool_response.rss_fetch to stay within the receiving model context window. Beginning and end are preserved.] \n \n suer)"}, {"fetched_at": "2026-09-23T04:34:39.610805+00:00", "id": "urn:tag:sec.gov,2008:accession-number=0001193125-26-398284", "link": "https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2029485/000119312526398284/0001193125-26-398284-index.htm", "published_at": "2026-09-22T20:34:49-04:00", "source": "https://www.sec.gov/cgi-bin/browse-edgar", "summary": "<b>Filed:</b> 2026-09-22 <b>AccNo:</b> 0001193125-26-398284 <b>Size:</b> 21 KB", "title": "4 - Kim Vyacheslav (0002029485) (Reporting)"}, {"fetched_at": "2026-09-23T04:34:39.610805+00:00", "id": "urn:tag:sec.gov,2008:accession-number=0001193125-26-398284", "link": "https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1985487/000119312526398284/0001193125-26-398284-index.htm", "published_at": "2026-09-22T20:34:49-04:00", "source": "https://www.sec.gov/cgi-bin/browse-edgar", "summary": "<b>Filed:</b> 2026-09-22 <b>AccNo:</b> 0001193125-26-398284 <b>Size:</b> 21 KB", "title": "4 - Joint Stock Co Kaspi.kz (0001985487) (Issuer)"}, {"fetched_at": "2026-09-23T04:34:39.610805+00:00", "id": "urn:tag:sec.gov,2008:accession-number=0001234544-26-000002", "link": "https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1260221/000123454426000002/0001234544-26-000002-index.htm", "published_at": "2026-09-22T20:24:06-04:00", "source": "https://www.sec.gov/cgi-bin/browse-edgar", "summary": "<b>Filed:</b> 2026-09-22 <b>AccNo:</b> 0001234544-26-000002 <b>Size:</b> 7 KB", "title": "4/A - TransDigm Group INC (0001260221) (Issuer)"}], "entry_count": 40, "etag": null, "feed": {"description": null, "link": null, "title": "Latest Filings - Wed, 23 Sep 2026 00:34:39 EDT"}, "fetched_at": "2026-09-23T04:34:39.610805+00:00", "id": "134eb45fb82ce12cab4f648a0fc8f49707c58f955253a2cbbdf8db0a50ae216e", "last_modified": null, "not_modified": false, "ok": true, "published_at": "2026-09-23T04:34:39+00:00", "source": "https://www.sec.gov/cgi-bin/browse-edgar", "url": "https://www.sec.gov/cgi-bin/browse-edgar"}; lumibot_tool_result_pruned=True; message=Tool response was shortened by Lumibot before sending it back to this model because the provider context window would otherwise be exceeded. Call a targeted tool again if more detail is required.; original_chars=19687; tool_name=rss_fetch
2026-09-23 00:34:41,088 | INFO | [sec-insider] Wrote parquet artifact: agent_detail:bear
2026-09-23 00:34:41,094 | INFO | [sec-insider] [agents] name=bear mode=backtesting model=gemini-3.5-flash-lite cache_hit=False tokens_in=54421 tokens_out=616 tokens_cached_in=40340 tokens_uncached_in=14081 tokens_thinking=0 tokens_total=55037 latency_ms=4581 first_event_latency_ms=1261 tool_calls=2 observability_warnings=0 summary='As the designated bear agent (`bear`), my objective is to argue the risks and structural weaknesses of blindly copying insider filings (such as Form 4 open-market transactions or related disclosures) as a trading strategy.  Based on the runtime context and the current simulated datetime (`2026-01-06T09:30:00-05:00`), as well as the research evidence confirming that there are zero valid open-market Form 4 filings available prior to or at the simulated date (with incoming feed entries dated September 2026, which are correctly filtered out as future data), blindly relying on or attempting to copy insider transactions presents severe structural and execution risks:  1. **Grants, Awards, and Non-Open-Market Acquisitions:**    Many reported Form 4 transactions do not represent discretionary open-market purchases by insiders. Instead, they frequently involve stock grants, restricted stock unit (RSU) vestings, option exercises, or automatic award distributions. Insiders receive these as compensation rather than active bets on immediate undervaluation. Copying these transactions as bullish trading signals fundamentally misinterprets mandatory corporate compensation as an opportunistic alpha signal.  2. **Amendments and Retractions:**    Form 4 filings are notoriously prone to amendments (Form 4/A). Insiders, company legal teams, or brokers frequently correct miscalculated share counts, transaction prices, or ownership classifications days or weeks after initial submission. A strategy that attempts to mechanically trade the initial filing timestamp is exposed to sudden execution or directional risk if the underlying filing is later amended or retracted.  3. **Thin Names and Liquidity Constraints:**    Insiders of small-cap, micro-cap, or thinly traded biotech and technology companies often file significant insider transactions. However, these names suffer from exceptionally wide bid-ask spreads, low daily trading volumes, and high price impact. Attempting to "piggyback" on insider buying in thin names leads to severe slippage, making it mathematically difficult or impossible to replicate insider fill prices.  4. **Sales and Discretionary Liquidity vs. Bearish Signals:**    While insider buying is often viewed as a positive signal, insider sales are frequently motivated by personal liquidity needs, estate planning, diversification, or tax withholding (e.g., covering RSU vestings) rather than a bearish outlook on the company\'s fundamentals. Treating every insider sale as an immediate short or exit signal risks cutting winning positions prematurely based on routine personal financial management.  RESULT: I analyzed the SEC Form 4 filing environment as of 2026-01-06, confirmed that no valid open-market filings are present prior to the simulated datetime, and outlined the key structural risks (grants, amendments, thin names, and sales) against blindly copying insider filings. No orders were placed.' trace=/Users/robertgrzesik/Library/Caches/lumibot/1.0/agent_runtime/traces/bear/2d3310083c2cefdc5d50f743e2950de260ca4077687f1f1e5627eebe0dbbb4fc-20260923043441075652.json
